 than I'll probably through YouTube.

21
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:00,120
 OK, so let's move forward.

22
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:01,840
 So we're going to be looking at the muscles that

23
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:03,200
 are crossing the shoulder joint.

24
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:05,480
 And these are responsible for the movements of your arm.

25
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:07,440
 Remember, that's the humer's bone.

26
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:09,680
 So again, going back to the shoulder joint.

27
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:12,600
 So you guys remember that the shoulder joint,

28
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,200
 it's a ball and socket joint.

29
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:16,840
 And this is the most flexible joint

30
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:18,280
 that we have in our body.

31
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:21,640
 But because it's the most flexible joint,

32
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:23,360
 it's not very stable.

33
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:25,320
 So it's a highly instable joint.

34
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:27,520
 Several muscles across each shoulder joint

35
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:29,120
 to insert onto the humer's.

36
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:31,040
 All the muscles that are acting on the humer's

37
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:33,080
 originate from the petrol girdle.

38
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:35,120
 However, we have two of these muscles,

39
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:37,120
 the latimis dorsi and the pectoralis

40
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:40,240
 major, which originate on the axial skeleton.

41
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:43,240
 So up to nine muscles that cross the shoulder joint.

42
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:46,040
 Only the pectoralis major, the latissimus dorsi,

43
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:49,240
 and the deltoid muscles are the primulis of arm movement.

44
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:52,000
 The remaining muscles are synergies and fixators.

45
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,520
 Now, four of these muscles, the supraspinatus, infraspinatus,

46
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:58,120
 terrace minor, and the subscapularis,

47
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:00,040
 they make up the rotator cuff muscles.

48
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:02,040
 They originate on the scapula and their tendons

49
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:03,720
 they blend in with the fibrous capsule

50
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:05,920
 of the shoulder joint on their way to the humerus.

51
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:08,200
 While the rotator cuff muscles act as synergists

52
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:10,720
 in the angular and rotational movements of the arm,

53
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:12,840
 their main function is to reinforce the capsule

54
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:15,800
 of the shoulder joint to prevent dislocation of the humerus.

55
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:17,400
 The other two muscles, the terrace major

56
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:18,920
 and the chorarchal brachialis,

57
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:20,360
 while they cross the shoulder joint,

58
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:22,720
 they don't contribute to its reinforcement.

59
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:24,640
 Generally speaking, muscles that originate

60
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:26,120
 anterior to the shoulder joint,

61
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:27,960
 so we're talking about the pectoralis major,

62
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:30,160
 the chorarchal brachialis and the anterior fibers

63
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:32,120
 of the deltoid, they flex the arm,

64
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:34,560
 so like when you're lifting something anteriorly,

65
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,120
 these are the muscles that play.

66
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:38,320
 Now, the prime mover of arm flexion

67
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:40,200
 is the pectoralis major.

68
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:41,720
 The biceps brachia of the arm,

69
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:43,400
 it also assists in this action.

70
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:45,880
 Muscles are originating posterior to the shoulder joint,

71
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:48,640
 they extend the arm, these include your latissimus dorsi,

72
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:50,680
 the posterior fibers of the deltoid muscles

73
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:52,120
 and the terrace major.

74
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:54,200
 The latissimus dorsi and pectoralis muscles

75
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:55,560
 are antagonist of one other

76
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:57,680
 in the flexion and sanction movements of the arm.

77
00:02:57,680 --> 00:02:59,280
 The middle region of the deltoid muscle,

78
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:02,120
 which extends over the super lateral side of the humerus,

79
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:04,920
 is a prime mover of arm abduction.

80
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:08,080
 The main arm abductors are the pectoralis major anteriorly

81
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:10,600
 and the latissimus dorsi posteriorly.

82
00:03:10,600 --> 00:03:12,520
 So here we have an illustration

83
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:15,360
 that's showing you the shoulder joint.

84
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:17,120
 And this first muscle that we're gonna be looking at

85
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:18,520
 is this pectoralis major.

86
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:20,320
 So this is a large fan shaped muscle

87
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:22,640
 that's covering the superior portion of the chest.

88
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:25,160
 It forms the anterior axillary fold

89
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:29,360
 and it's divided into equivicular and sternal parts.

90
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:31,600
 The origin is gonna be the sternal end of the clavicle,

91
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:34,400
 the sternum, the cartlages of ribs one through six

92
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:36,760
 and sometimes seven, and the aponeurosis

93
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:38,440
 of the external oblique muscles.

94
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:40,520
 As for the insertion, the fibers they converge

95
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:41,920
 to insert by a short tendon

96
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:43,680
 into the intertubercular sulcus

97
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:46,000
 and the greater tubercle of the humerus.

98
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,240
 As for the actions, this is a prime mover

99
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:49,600
 of arm flexion.

100
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:51,880
 It rotates the arms medially

101
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:55,520
 and it also adducts the arms against resistance.

102
00:03:55,520 --> 00:03:58,360
 Now with the scapula and the arms in a fixed position,

103
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:00,120
 it pulls the ribcage upwards.

104
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:02,720
 So this helps you in climbing and throwing

105
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:05,360
 and pushing and enforced inspiration.

106
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:07,440
 Now this thick multi-painite muscle

107
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:10,600
 that's forms the rounded shoulder masses, the deltoid.

108
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:12,840
 And this is a site that's commonly used

109
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:15,720
 for administering intramuscular injections,

110
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:19,440
 specifically in men because men typically have a larger,

111
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:21,560
 more prominent deltoid than in females.

112
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:24,840
 The other place that you give injections

113
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:29,080
 is the gluteus maximus, your butt muscle.

114
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:32,080
 Its insertion is a deltoid tuberosity of the humerus

115
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:34,560
 and as for the origin, it embraces the insertion

116
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:37,600
 of the trapezius, the lateral third of the clavicle,

117
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:40,840
 in addition to the chromium and spine of the scapula.

118
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:42,680
 It's innervated by the axillary nerve

119
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:44,280
 and as for its actions,

120
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:46,560
 when all the fibers contract simultaneously,

121
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:49,760
 it acts as the prime mover of arm abduction.

122
00:04:49,760 --> 00:04:51,720
 It's an antagonist of the petros major

123
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:54,640
 in the latissimus dorsi, which adduct the arm.

124
00:04:54,640 --> 00:04:56,720
 Now if only the anterior fibers are active,

125
00:04:56,720 --> 00:05:00,040
 it can actually act as a powerful muscle inflection

126
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:02,040
 and medial rotation of the humerus.

127
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:05,120
 So because of that, it's said to be a synergies

128
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:07,240
 of the petros major as well.

129
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:09,360
 Now if only a posterior fibers are active,

130
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:12,080
 it causes extension and lateral rotation of the arm.

131
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:13,960
 This muscle is also active when we're walking

132
00:05:13,960 --> 00:05:15,240
 and our arms are swinging.

133
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:17,560
 Now we're gonna be looking at this broad, flat,

134
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:19,400
 triangular muscle of our lower back,

135
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:21,280
 which you find around our lumbar region.

136
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:23,400
 This is the latissimus dorsi.

137
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:26,080
 It's covered by the trapezius superiorly

138
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:29,040
 and it contributes to the posterior wall of the axilla.

139
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:31,280
 Its insertion is gonna be the intertuberculic groove

140
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:34,680
 of the humerus and as for the origin,

141
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:35,960
 there's gonna be several sites.

142
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:39,160
 So we have the spinous process of T7 all the way down

143
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:41,840
 to extending down to L5 of the vertebrae,

144
00:05:41,840 --> 00:05:46,000
 the iliic crest of the sacrum, the thoracol lumbar fascia,

145
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,080
 the inferior angle of the scapula,

146
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:49,880
 and the lower three or four ribs.

147
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:51,440
 It's innervated by the thoracol door.

148
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:54,200
 So nerves, so specifically we're looking at cervical nerves,

149
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:57,320
 six cervical nerves, seven and cervical nerve, eight.

150
00:05:57,320 --> 00:06:00,200
 The subscapularis is a rotator cuff muscle.

151
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:02,880
 It forms part of the posterior wall of the axilla

152
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:05,320
 and its tendon of insertion passes in front

153
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:06,440
 of the shoulder joint.

154
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:09,160
 Its insertion is a lesser tubercle of the humerus

155
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:11,800
 and as for the origin, it's a subscapular fossa

156
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:12,840
 of the scapula.

157
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:14,800
 It's innervated by the subscapular nerves.

158
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:17,240
 So we're looking at cervical nerves, five cervical nerves,

159
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:19,040
 six and cervical nerves, seven.

160
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:20,840
 As far as for its actions go,

161
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:23,360
 it's a chief medial rotator of the arm.

162
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:25,280
 It's assisted by the pectoralis major

163
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:27,160
 and it helps hold the head of the humerus

164
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:29,680
 in the glenoid cavity stabilizing the shoulder joint.

165
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:32,480
 The supraspinatus muscle, which is a rotator cuff muscle,

166
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:34,120
 which runs deep to the trapezius,

167
00:06:34,120 --> 00:06:37,760
 is named for its location on the posterior aspect of the scapula.

168
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:40,720
 Its origin is a supraspinus fossa of the scapula

169
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:42,920
 and as for the insertion, it's a superior part

170
00:06:42,920 --> 00:06:45,200
 of the greater tubercle of the humerus.

171
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:47,600
 It's innervated by the supraspapular nerve

172
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:51,640
 and its prime action is to initiate abduction of the arms.

173
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:53,720
 It also helps stabilize the shoulder joint

174
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:56,560
 and it helps prevent downward dislocation of the humerus.

175
00:06:56,560 --> 00:06:58,080
 So when you're carrying something heavy,

176
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:00,600
 like a gallon of milk or a suitcase,

177
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:02,640
 this is one of the muscles that's helped preventing your arm

178
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:03,800
 from being dislocated.

179
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:05,880
 As you can see, the deltoid has been cut out

180
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:07,280
 and because it's been cut out,

181
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:08,840
 we can see this one next muscle here,

182
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:10,160
 the infraspinatus.

183
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:11,880
 So it's partially covered by the deltoid

184
00:07:11,880 --> 00:07:14,000
 and the trapezius as well.

185
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,160
 It's named for its scapular location

186
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:18,760
 and it's also another one of the rotator cuff muscles.

187
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:21,960
 Its origin is gonna be the infraspinus fossa of the scapula

188
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:24,400
 and the insertion is gonna be the greater tubercle

189
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:27,040
 of the humerus posterior to the insertion

190
00:07:27,040 --> 00:07:28,400
 of the supraspinatus.

191
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:29,680
 Like the supraspinatus,

192
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:32,640
 it's also innervated by the supraspapular nerve.

193
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:34,960
 As for its actions, primary action is to rotate

194
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:37,680
 the arms laterally and it also helps hold the head

195
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:38,960
 of the humerus in the glenoid cavity.

196
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:41,220
 Again, to further stabilize the shoulder joint.

197
00:07:41,220 --> 00:07:43,120
 The next rotator cuff muscle we're gonna be looking at

198
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:44,960
 is the terrace minor muscle.

199
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:47,080
 And this is a small elongated muscle.

200
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:48,800
 It lies inferior to the infraspinatus

201
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:50,360
 and sometimes it may be inseparable.

202
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:52,040
 Its origin is the lateral border

203
00:07:52,040 --> 00:07:53,800
 of the dorsal scapular surface

204
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:55,920
 and for the insertion, it's a greater tubercle

205
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:59,600
 of the humerus inferior to the infraspinatus insertion.

206
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:00,760
 As far as its actions go,

207
00:08:00,760 --> 00:08:02,680
 they're gonna be the same as the infraspinatus muscles.

208
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:05,000
 We're talking about rotating the arm laterally

209
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,200
 and it's innervated by the axillary nerve.

210
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:09,240
 Next, we move on to the terrace major.

211
00:08:09,240 --> 00:08:11,080
 And this is a thick rounded muscle

212
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:13,120
 it's located inferior to the terrace minor

213
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:15,520
 and it helps form the posterior wall of the axilla

214
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:18,960
 along with latsysimus dorsi and the subscapularis.

215
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:21,480
 Its origin is the posterior surface of the scapula

216
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:24,360
 at the inferior angle and its insertion is the crust

217
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:27,880
 of the lesser tubercle on the anterior humerus.

218
00:08:27,880 --> 00:08:30,000
 Its insertion tendon, it gets fused

219
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:31,640
 with that of the latsysimus dorsi.

220
00:08:31,640 --> 00:08:33,440
 Its prime actions include extending,

221
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:36,360
 medially rotating and adducting the arm.

222
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:39,040
 It's also synergis of the latsysimus dorsi.

223
00:08:39,040 --> 00:08:41,600
 It's innervated by the lower subscapular nerve

224
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:43,120
 so we're looking at cervical nerve six

225
00:08:43,120 --> 00:08:44,440
 and cervical nerve seven.

226
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:46,760
 Finally, we come to the chorocal brachialis

227
00:08:46,760 --> 00:08:49,320
 and this is a small cylindrical muscle.

228
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:52,240
 Its origin is the chorocoid process of the scapula

229
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:56,120
 and it inserts into the medial surface of the humerus shaft.

230
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:58,600
 Its actions to flex and adduct the arms

231
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:00,760
 and it's the synergis of the petrelis major

232
00:09:00,760 --> 00:09:03,680
 and it's innervated by the muscular cutaneous nerve.

233
