 And again, yet another one.

258
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:19,760
 And here's one more.

259
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:22,440
 And I think this is the last one

260
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:23,560
 for the glenohumeral joint.

261
00:10:23,560 --> 00:10:25,840
 So please be sure you view all of these.

262
00:10:25,840 --> 00:10:28,760
 Again, you can find them at the Pearson website

263
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:31,320
 or the DVD that came with your book

264
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:34,240
 in addition to that, you can search for it on YouTube.

265
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:35,400
 Now we're gonna be looking at muscles

266
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:36,760
 that are crossing the elbow joint

267
00:10:36,760 --> 00:10:39,440
 for a flexion and extension of the forearm.

268
00:10:39,440 --> 00:10:43,520
 So muscles that are standing out across the upper arm

269
00:10:43,520 --> 00:10:46,280
 and going to the elbow joint,

270
00:10:46,280 --> 00:10:48,400
 they insert on the forearm bones.

271
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:50,600
 Now since the elbow is a hinge joint,

272
00:10:50,600 --> 00:10:52,400
 movements promoted by these arm muscles,

273
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:54,760
 they're limited almost entirely to flexion

274
00:10:54,760 --> 00:10:56,560
 and extension of the forearm.

275
00:10:56,560 --> 00:10:57,720
 Walls of the fascia,

276
00:10:57,720 --> 00:11:00,360
 they divide the arm into two muscle compartments.

277
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:04,600
 We have the posterior extensors and the anterior flexors.

278
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:07,360
 The main forearm extensor is a triceps brachii muscle,

279
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:09,360
 which forms nearly the entire musculature

280
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:10,840
 or the posterior compartment.

281
00:11:10,840 --> 00:11:12,520
 All the anterior arm muscles,

282
00:11:12,520 --> 00:11:14,600
 they flex the forearm at the elbow joint.

283
00:11:14,600 --> 00:11:17,320
 And these are the brachialis, the biceps brachii

284
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:19,520
 and the brachior radialis muscles.

285
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:21,320
 Now the brachialis and the biceps,

286
00:11:21,320 --> 00:11:24,640
 they insert into the ulna and the radius respectively.

287
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:27,560
 And they contract simultaneously during flexion.

288
00:11:27,560 --> 00:11:29,880
 And these are the chief forearm flexors.

289
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:32,400
 Just about everybody is familiar with the biceps brachii muscle

290
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:34,080
 because when we flex our forearm,

291
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:35,800
 this is the muscle that bulges out.

292
00:11:35,800 --> 00:11:38,920
 Now the one that's not as well known is the brachialis.

293
00:11:38,920 --> 00:11:41,120
 Now this lies deep to the biceps,

294
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:44,200
 but it says equally as important during the flexion.

295
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:46,600
 So it's a synergist of the biceps brachii.

296
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:49,480
 The brachior radialis also helps flex the arm,

297
00:11:49,480 --> 00:11:51,480
 but it's a weak forearm flexor.

298
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:53,960
 Now the only muscle that we find on the posterior compartment

299
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:57,680
 of the arm is this large, fleshy triceps brachii muscle.

300
00:11:57,680 --> 00:11:59,640
 It has three-headed origin.

301
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:01,000
 The long and the lateral heads,

302
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,200
 they lie superficial to the medial head.

303
00:12:03,200 --> 00:12:05,400
 So for the origin of the long head,

304
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:08,160
 it's the infraglinoid tubercle of the scapula.

305
00:12:08,160 --> 00:12:10,160
 The lateral head, we find the insertion

306
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:12,240
 on the posterior shaft of the humerus.

307
00:12:12,240 --> 00:12:15,520
 And for the medial head, it's the posterior humeral shaft

308
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:17,200
 distal to the radial groove.

309
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:20,040
 As for the insertion, it's by the common tendon

310
00:12:20,040 --> 00:12:22,680
 that goes into the lurkman process of the ulna.

311
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:25,560
 It's prime action is a powerful forearm extensor

312
00:12:25,560 --> 00:12:28,320
 and it's an antagonist of the forearm flexors.

313
00:12:28,320 --> 00:12:30,600
 The long and lateral heads, they're mainly active

314
00:12:30,600 --> 00:12:32,760
 in extension against resistance

315
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:35,040
 and it's innervated by the radial nerve.

316
00:12:35,040 --> 00:12:37,640
 The anconias, which is a short triangle in muscles,

317
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:39,200
 partially blended with the distal end

318
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:42,480
 of the triceps on the posterior aspect of the humerus.

319
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:45,440
 Its origin is a lateral epicondyle of the humerus

320
00:12:45,440 --> 00:12:47,360
 and its insertion is a lateral aspect

321
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:49,800
 of the ulnar crown process of the ulna.

322
00:12:49,800 --> 00:12:53,200
 Its primary action is to control ulnar abduction

323
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:54,800
 during forearm pronation.

324
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:57,400
 And it's also a synergist of the triceps brachii

325
00:12:57,400 --> 00:12:59,240
 during elbow extensions.

326
00:12:59,240 --> 00:13:01,480
 And as far as this nurse supply goes,

327
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:03,320
 it's innervated by the radial nerve.

328
00:13:03,320 --> 00:13:06,160
 So these are the two posterior muscles of the elbow joint.

329
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:08,280
 So be sure to watch this video over here

330
00:13:08,280 --> 00:13:10,120
 for the triceps brachii muscle.

331
00:13:10,120 --> 00:13:11,520
 Again, go to the Pearson website

332
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:14,880
 or the DVD that came with your book

333
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:16,680
 in addition to searching on YouTube.

334
00:13:16,680 --> 00:13:20,760
 And now we're gonna be looking at the anterior muscles

335
00:13:20,760 --> 00:13:22,560
 of the elbow joint.

336
00:13:22,560 --> 00:13:24,880
 So we start off with this biceps brachii muscle.

337
00:13:24,880 --> 00:13:27,080
 It's a two-headed fusiform muscle.

338
00:13:27,080 --> 00:13:30,560
 The bellies they unite as the insertion point is approached.

339
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:31,680
 At the tendon of the long head,

340
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:34,080
 it also helps to stabilize the shoulder joint.

341
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:36,600
 The origin for the short head is the core quad process.

342
00:13:36,600 --> 00:13:39,040
 And for the long head is a super glenoid tubercle

343
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:41,120
 and the lip of the glenoid cavity.

344
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:42,200
 The tendon of the long head,

345
00:13:42,200 --> 00:13:43,440
 it runs within the capsule

346
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:47,160
 and into the intertrovicular sulcus of the humerus.

347
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:48,920
 The insertion is by a common tendon

348
00:13:48,920 --> 00:13:50,760
 into the radial tuberosity.

349
00:13:50,760 --> 00:13:52,880
 Its prime action is to flex the elbow joint

350
00:13:52,880 --> 00:13:54,480
 and supinate the forearms.

351
00:13:54,480 --> 00:13:57,160
 These actions, they usually occur at the same time.

352
00:13:57,160 --> 00:13:59,720
 So when you're taking a bottle of olive oil

353
00:13:59,720 --> 00:14:01,440
 and you're pulling the cork out.

354
00:14:01,440 --> 00:14:03,320
 So as you pull the cork out,

355
00:14:03,320 --> 00:14:04,760
 think about when you're turning the cork

356
00:14:04,760 --> 00:14:07,240
 and then pulling back up and flexing the arm.

357
00:14:07,240 --> 00:14:08,840
 This is the action that's happening

358
00:14:08,840 --> 00:14:11,400
 and provided by this biceps brachii muscle.

359
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:13,800
 And it's innervated by the muscular cutaneous nerve.

360
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:16,160
 The brachii out of muscle is a strong muscle

361
00:14:16,160 --> 00:14:18,480
 that's immediately deep to the biceps brachii

362
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:20,080
 on the distal humerus.

363
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:22,400
 Its origin is the front of the distal humerus

364
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:25,080
 and it embraces the insertion of the deltoid muscle.

365
00:14:25,080 --> 00:14:28,400
 For the insertion is the coronite process of the ulna

366
00:14:28,400 --> 00:14:30,400
 and the capsule of the elbow joint.

367
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:31,720
 Like the biceps brachii,

368
00:14:31,720 --> 00:14:34,280
 it's also innervated by the muscular cutaneous nerve.

369
00:14:34,280 --> 00:14:37,800
 And for its action, it's a major forearm flexor.

370
00:14:37,800 --> 00:14:40,440
 It lifts the ulna as a biceps lifts the radius.

371
00:14:40,440 --> 00:14:42,760
 Now we'll look at the brachioradialis muscle.

372
00:14:42,760 --> 00:14:45,320
 This is a superficial muscle of the lateral forearm.

373
00:14:45,320 --> 00:14:48,000
 It forms a lateral boundary of the cuboidal fossa

374
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:49,960
 and it extends from the distal humerus

375
00:14:49,960 --> 00:14:51,560
 to the distal forearm.

376
00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:54,360
 Its origin is the lateral supercondola ridge

377
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:56,000
 at the distal end of the humerus

378
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,600
 and the insertion is the base of the styloid process

379
00:14:58,600 --> 00:14:59,800
 of the radius.

380
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:01,720
 It's a synergist and forearm flexion

381
00:15:01,720 --> 00:15:04,080
 and it works best when the forearm is partially flexed

382
00:15:04,080 --> 00:15:05,480
 and semi-pronated.

383
00:15:05,480 --> 00:15:07,040
 It also stabilizes the elbow

384
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:09,280
 during rapid flexion and extension.

385
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:11,000
 It's innervated by the radial nerve

386
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:12,240
 and this is an exception

387
00:15:12,240 --> 00:15:15,200
 as the radial nerve typically serves extensor muscles.

388
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:16,800
 So be sure to watch this animation

389
00:15:16,800 --> 00:15:18,320
 on the biceps brachii.

390
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:21,080
 Here's another one for the brachialis

391
00:15:21,080 --> 00:15:24,040
 and another for the brachioradialis.

392
00:15:24,040 --> 00:15:28,320
 And there's an overview of the elbow joint in the forearm

393
00:15:28,320 --> 00:15:30,920
 and this one's for the muscles of the elbow joint

394
00:15:30,920 --> 00:15:34,000
 and looks like one more for muscles of the elbow joint.

395
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,560
 And we have another for the elbow joint.

396
00:15:36,560 --> 00:15:40,440
 So please be sure to view view of these animations.

397
00:15:40,440 --> 00:15:42,920
 You can expect questions on the exam

398
00:15:42,920 --> 00:15:45,040
 from these animations is highly likely.

399
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:48,320
 So please be sure to look out for these.

400
00:15:48,320 --> 00:15:51,120
 Again, go to the website for Pearson.

401
00:15:51,120 --> 00:15:53,920
 You can look for it on YouTube in addition to that.

402
00:15:53,920 --> 00:15:57,560
 If your book came with the DVD, look for it on that.

403
00:15:57,560 --> 00:15:59,760
 Now we're gonna be moving on to the muscles of the forearm

404
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:02,280
 and these are responsible for movements of your wrist,

405
00:16:02,280 --> 00:16:04,200
 your hands and fingers.

406
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:07,520
 The muscles of the forearm perform several basic functions.

407
00:16:07,520 --> 00:16:10,080
 Some cause wrist movements, others move the fingers

408
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:13,440
 and thumbs and a few help pronate and supinate the forearm.

409
00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:15,480
 In most cases, they're fleshy portions,

410
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:18,480
 they contribute to the roundness of the proximal forearm

411
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:21,280
 and then they taper to long tendons distally

412
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:23,040
 to insert into the hand.

413
00:16:23,040 --> 00:16:25,760
 At the wrist, these tendons, they're securely anchored

414
00:16:25,760 --> 00:16:28,200
 by a band-like dignity of deep fascia

415
00:16:28,200 --> 00:16:31,960
 that's called flexor and extensor retiniculae.

416
00:16:31,960 --> 00:16:33,200
 These bands at the wrist,

417
00:16:33,200 --> 00:16:36,200
 they keep the tendons from jumping outwards when tensed.

418
00:16:36,200 --> 00:16:37,960
 Crowd it together in the wrist and palm,

419
00:16:37,960 --> 00:16:40,680
 the muscle tendons are surrounded by slippery tendon she said,

420
00:16:40,680 --> 00:16:43,040
 minimize friction as they slide against one another.

421
00:16:43,040 --> 00:16:45,080
 The forearm muscles are subdivided by fascia

422
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:46,360
 into two main compartments,

423
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:48,800
 the anterior flexors and the posterior extensors,

424
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:51,160
 each with superficial and deep muscle layers.

425
00:16:51,160 --> 00:16:54,120
 So the anterior muscles, they're flexors,

426
00:16:54,120 --> 00:16:56,600
 they insert via the flexor retiniculum

427
00:16:56,600 --> 00:16:58,960
 and the posterior muscles, which are the extensors,

428
00:16:58,960 --> 00:17:01,960
 they insert play the extensor retiniculum.

429
00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:03,960
 Most flexors in the anterior compartment

430
00:17:03,960 --> 00:17:06,080
 arise from a common tendon on the humerus

431
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:08,680
 and are innervated largely by the median nerve.

432
00:17:08,680 --> 00:17:11,680
 The two anterior compartment muscles are not flexors

433
00:17:11,680 --> 00:17:14,120
 but pronators, the pronator terrace

434
00:17:14,120 --> 00:17:15,960
 and the pronator quadratus.

435
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:19,200
 And pronation is one of the most important forearm movements.

436
00:17:19,200 --> 00:17:20,800
 Muscles of the posterior compartment

437
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:22,800
 they extend the wrist and fingers.

438
00:17:22,800 --> 00:17:24,560
 One exception is a supinator muscle,

439
00:17:24,560 --> 00:17:27,600
 which assists the biceps brachii muscle of the arm

440
00:17:27,600 --> 00:17:29,520
 in supinating the forearm.

441
00:17:29,520 --> 00:17:32,080
 Of the eight muscles of the anterior compartment,

442
00:17:32,080 --> 00:17:35,240
 five of them are superficial and three of them are deep.

443
00:17:35,240 --> 00:17:37,800
 Most of them they arise from a common flexor tendon

444
00:17:37,800 --> 00:17:40,640
 and they're attached to the medial epicondyle of the humerus

445
00:17:40,640 --> 00:17:43,160
 and they have additional origins as well.

446
00:17:43,160 --> 00:17:45,400
 Most of the tendons and insertion of these flexors

447
00:17:45,400 --> 00:17:46,680
 they're held in place at the wrist

448
00:17:46,680 --> 00:17:50,000
 by a thickening of deep fascia called the flexor retiniculum.

449
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:53,160
 First we're gonna be looking at the pronator terrace muscle.

450
00:17:53,160 --> 00:17:54,720
 This is the two-hydrous muscles

451
00:17:54,720 --> 00:17:56,920
 and when we're looking at it in a superficial view

452
00:17:56,920 --> 00:17:58,720
 you can find it between the proximal margins

453
00:17:58,720 --> 00:18:02,520
 of the brachii radialis and the flexor carpi radialis.

454
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:05,520
 It forms a medial boundary of the cuboidal fossa.

455
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:07,800
 It's origins and medial epicondyle of the humerus

456
00:18:07,800 --> 00:18:10,040
 and the coronite process of the ulna.

457
00:18:10,040 --> 00:18:12,160
 It's insertions by a common tendon,

458
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:15,120
 mid shafts on the lateral aspect of the radius.

459
00:18:15,120 --> 00:18:17,240
 It's prime action is to pronate the forearm

460
00:18:17,240 --> 00:18:19,720
 and it's also a weak flexor of the elbow.

461
00:18:19,720 --> 00:18:21,600
 It's innervated by the median nerve.

462
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:24,320
 Next we look at the flexor carpi radialis muscle

463
00:18:24,320 --> 00:18:26,800
 and this runs diagonally across the forearm.

464
00:18:26,800 --> 00:18:29,560
 Midway it's fleshy belly, it's replaced by a flat tendon

465
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:31,520
 that becomes cord-like at the wrist.

466
00:18:31,520 --> 00:18:33,640
 So you can see that over here.

467
00:18:33,640 --> 00:18:36,600
 It's origin is the medial epicondyle of the humerus

468
00:18:36,600 --> 00:18:37,920
 and it's insertion is the base

469
00:18:37,920 --> 00:18:40,120
 of the second and third metacarpals.

470
00:18:40,120 --> 00:18:42,000
 The insertion tendons easily seen

471
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:43,880
 and it provides as a guide to the position

472
00:18:43,880 --> 00:18:45,880
 of the radial artery and this is useful

473
00:18:45,880 --> 00:18:49,120
 when taking a pulse and also in obtaining blood gases

474
00:18:49,120 --> 00:18:52,280
 when you're taking what's called an arterial blood gas.

475
00:18:52,280 --> 00:18:54,040
 This is one of the landmarks that you use.

476
00:18:54,040 --> 00:18:57,200
 You wanna go lateral to that tendon when you feel it.

477
00:18:57,200 --> 00:18:59,600
 It's prime action is as a powerful flexor of the wrist

478
00:18:59,600 --> 00:19:02,960
 and it abducts the hand and it's also weak synergies

479
00:19:02,960 --> 00:19:04,340
 of elbow flexion.

480
00:19:04,340 --> 00:19:06,500
 It's also innervated by the median nerve.

481
00:19:06,500 --> 00:19:08,920
 The palm is longest which is a small fleshy muscle

482
00:19:08,920 --> 00:19:10,640
 with a long insertion tendon.

483
00:19:10,640 --> 00:19:12,800
 It may be used as a guide to find the median nerve

484
00:19:12,800 --> 00:19:14,680
 that lies lateral to it at the wrist.

485
00:19:14,680 --> 00:19:17,400
 It's origin is the medial epicondyle of the humerus

486
00:19:17,400 --> 00:19:20,680
 and the insertion is the palm or aponeurosis.

487
00:19:20,680 --> 00:19:22,920
 It's also innervated by the median nerve.

488
00:19:22,920 --> 00:19:25,160
 Its prime action is to tense the skin and fascia

489
00:19:25,160 --> 00:19:27,280
 of the palm during hand movements.

490
00:19:27,280 --> 00:19:29,520
 It's also weak wrist flexor in addition

491
00:19:29,520 --> 00:19:31,960
 to being a weak synergist for elbow flexion

492
00:19:31,960 --> 00:19:34,280
 and it's also innervated by the median nerve.

493
00:19:34,280 --> 00:19:35,800
 Next we're gonna be looking at this flexor

494
00:19:35,800 --> 00:19:37,400
 carbide ulnerus muscle.

495
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:38,440
 It's a two-headed muscle

496
00:19:38,440 --> 00:19:40,800
 and it's the most medial muscle of the group.

497
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:43,840
 The ulnar nerve also lies lateral to its tendon.

498
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:46,360
 The origin is the medial epicondyle of the humerus

499
00:19:46,360 --> 00:19:47,840
 and the olecranon process

500
00:19:47,840 --> 00:19:49,600
 and posterior surface of the ulnar.

501
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:52,360
 The insertion is gonna be the pisiform and the hemate bones

502
00:19:52,360 --> 00:19:55,000
 and base of the fifth metacarpal.

503
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:56,280
 The prime action for this muscle

504
00:19:56,280 --> 00:19:58,320
 is that it's a powerful flexor of the wrist

505
00:19:58,320 --> 00:19:59,760
 and it also adducts the hand

506
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:02,440
 in concert with the extensor carbide ulnerus.

507
00:20:02,440 --> 00:20:03,280
 In addition to that,

508
00:20:03,280 --> 00:20:06,240
 it also stabilizes the wrist during finger extensions

509
00:20:06,240 --> 00:20:08,680
 and it's innervated by the ulnar nerve.

510
00:20:08,680 --> 00:20:10,000
 And finally, we have the flexor

511
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,280
 digitorum superficialis muscle.

512
00:20:12,280 --> 00:20:15,000
 It's a two-headed muscle that's more deeply placed

513
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:16,760
 and it's overlain by muscles above

514
00:20:16,760 --> 00:20:19,600
 but it's still visible at the distal end of the forearm.

515
00:20:19,600 --> 00:20:20,840
 As far as origin,

516
00:20:20,840 --> 00:20:23,080
 it's the medial epicondyle of the humerus,

517
00:20:23,080 --> 00:20:24,600
 the cornered process of the ulnar

518
00:20:24,600 --> 00:20:26,840
 and the shaft of the radius.

519
00:20:26,840 --> 00:20:28,560
 It's insertion is by four tendons

520
00:20:28,560 --> 00:20:33,080
 into the middle flanges of fingers two, three, four, and five.

521
00:20:33,280 --> 00:20:34,960
 It's innervated by the median nerve

522
00:20:34,960 --> 00:20:37,200
 and its prime action is that it flexes the wrist

523
00:20:37,200 --> 00:20:40,840
 and middle flanges of fingers two, three, four, and five.

524
00:20:40,840 --> 00:20:42,720
 And this is an important finger flexor

525
00:20:42,720 --> 00:20:46,400
 when speed and flushing against resistance are required.

526
00:20:46,400 --> 00:20:49,040
 Now we're gonna be looking at the deep muscles of the forearm

527
00:20:49,040 --> 00:20:50,320
 and the first one we're gonna be looking at

528
00:20:50,320 --> 00:20:53,000
 is the flexor polysis longus

529
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:54,840
 and it's partially covered by the flexor

530
00:20:54,840 --> 00:20:56,640
 digitorum superficialis

531
00:20:56,640 --> 00:21:00,240
 and it parallels the flexor digitorum profundis laterally.

532
00:21:00,240 --> 00:21:02,360
 It's origin is the anterior surface of the radius

533
00:21:02,360 --> 00:21:03,840
 and the introsseous membrane

534
00:21:03,840 --> 00:21:06,400
 and the insertion is the distal failings of the thumb

535
00:21:06,400 --> 00:21:09,400
 and its prime action is to flex the distal failings of the thumb

536
00:21:09,400 --> 00:21:11,520
 and it's innervated by a branch of the median nerve.

537
00:21:11,520 --> 00:21:13,760
 So we're looking at cervical nerve eight

538
00:21:13,760 --> 00:21:15,360
 and the first thoracic nerve.

539
00:21:15,360 --> 00:21:18,160
 Now we're gonna be looking at the flexor digitorum profundis

540
00:21:18,160 --> 00:21:20,280
 and this muscle has extensive origins.

541
00:21:20,280 --> 00:21:22,080
 It's overlain entirely by the flexor

542
00:21:22,080 --> 00:21:24,440
 digitorum superficialis.

543
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:27,880
 The origins it has include the cornered process,

544
00:21:27,880 --> 00:21:30,280
 the anterior medial surface of the ulnar

545
00:21:30,280 --> 00:21:32,600
 and the introsseous membrane.

546
00:21:32,600 --> 00:21:34,680
 Its insertion is by four tendons

547
00:21:34,680 --> 00:21:38,840
 into the distal flanges of fingers two, three, four and five.

548
00:21:38,840 --> 00:21:41,120
 Its prime action is that it flexes the distal

549
00:21:41,120 --> 00:21:42,760
 interflangeal joints.

550
00:21:42,760 --> 00:21:46,080
 It's also a slow acting flexor of any or all the fingers

551
00:21:46,080 --> 00:21:48,560
 and it is this inflexing the wrist.

552
00:21:48,560 --> 00:21:50,200
 As to its nerve supply,

553
00:21:50,200 --> 00:21:52,480
 the medial half is innervated by the ulnar nerve

554
00:21:52,480 --> 00:21:55,200
 and the lateral half is innervated by the median nerve.

555
00:21:55,200 --> 00:21:57,720
 And lastly, we're at the pronator quadratus.

556
00:21:57,720 --> 00:22:00,000
 This is the deepest muscle of the distal forearm.

557
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
 It passes downward and laterally

558
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,120
 and this is the only muscle that arises solely

559
00:22:04,120 --> 00:22:06,920
 from the ulnar and inserts solely into the radius.

560
00:22:06,920 --> 00:22:10,160
 Its origin is a distal portion of the anterior ulnar shaft

561
00:22:10,160 --> 00:22:12,000
 and its insertion is a distal surface

562
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:13,720
 of the anterior radius.

563
00:22:13,720 --> 00:22:15,240
 It's innervated by the median nerve

564
00:22:15,240 --> 00:22:17,040
 and its action is that it's the prime move

565
00:22:17,040 --> 00:22:18,520
 of forearm pronation.

566
00:22:18,520 --> 00:22:20,600
 It also acts with the pronator teres

567
00:22:20,600 --> 00:22:23,240
 and it helps to hold the ulnar and radius together.

568
00:22:23,240 --> 00:22:25,040
 So be sure you view this muscle,

569
00:22:25,040 --> 00:22:27,640
 this video on the pronator teres

570
00:22:27,640 --> 00:22:30,960
 and this one on the fluxor carbide radialis.

571
00:22:30,960 --> 00:22:36,160
 We have another video here for the fluxor carbide ulnaris

572
00:22:36,160 --> 00:22:37,640
 and another one for the fluxor

573
00:22:37,640 --> 00:22:39,600
 digitorum superficialis.

574
00:22:39,600 --> 00:22:45,360
 And this is for the anterior muscles of the wrist

575
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:48,440
 and fingers and this is a continuation of that.

576
00:22:48,440 --> 00:22:50,880
 So please be sure you watch these videos.

